# Clean Git Worktree

Agents must leave a clean `git status` when finishing work, unless the user explicitly asks to keep WIP uncommitted.

## Before ending a task

1. Run `git status` and resolve any leftover changes from your work.
2. Stage and commit completed work in **logical sequential commits** with meaningful messages (why over what).
3. Do not end with unstaged/untracked files you created or modified as part of the task.

## What to commit vs ignore

- Commit only relevant source, config, and docs for the change.
- Respect `.gitignore`. Do not leave or commit untracked build artifacts (`dist/`, `target/`, `.next/`, coverage, caches, generated binaries).
- Never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, tokens, private keys). Warn if the user asks to commit them.

## Commit hygiene

```text
# ❌ BAD — finish with dirty tree after implementing a feature
# (modified files left unstaged / uncommitted)

# ✅ GOOD — stage relevant files, commit, verify clean
git add <relevant-files>
git commit -m "feat: describe why this change exists"
git status  # should be clean (or only unrelated pre-existing dirt)
```

- Prefer small, sequential commits over one catch-all dump when the work has distinct steps.
- Do not use `--no-verify` or amend pushed history unless the user explicitly requests it.
- If the tree was already dirty before your work, do not mix unrelated pre-existing changes into your commits; still commit your own completed work so you do not add more dirt.
